.TH TMUX-PANE 1 "2026-11-16" "tmux-tools" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tmux-pane \- type into one exact tmux pane, read it, and wait on it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tmux-pane send
.B \-t
.I %N
.RI [ SERVER ]
.RI [ ACTION ...]
.RB [ \-\-enter ]
.RB [ \-\-type ]
.RB [ \-\-keep\-mode ]
.RB [ \-\-print\-marker ]
.RB [ \-\-wait " agent|prompt"
.RI [ WAIT-OPTS ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-capture ]
.RB [ \-\-lines
.IR N ]
.RI [ READ-FMT ]]
.RB [ \-\- ]
.RI [ TEXT ...]
.br
.B tmux-pane read
.B \-t
.I %N
.RI [ SERVER ]
.RB [ \-\-lines
.I N
|
.B \-\-all
|
.B \-\-last\-output
.RB [ \-\-count
.IR N ]]
.RB [ \-\-since
.IR MARKER ]
.RI [ READ-FMT ]
.br
.B tmux-pane wait
.B \-t
.I %N
.RI [ SERVER ]
.B \-\-wait agent|prompt
.RB [ \-\-since
.IR MARKER ]
.RI [ WAIT-OPTS ]
.RB [ \-\-capture
.RB [ \-\-lines
.IR N ]
.RI [ READ-FMT ]]
.br
.B tmux-pane mark
.B \-t
.I %N
.RI [ SERVER ]
.br
.B tmux-pane
.RI [ subcommand ]
.B \-\-help
.br
.B tmux-pane \-\-interface
.br
.B tmux-pane \-\-check\-refs
.IR FILE ...
(a maintenance aid for documentation tests; see
.BR "INTERFACE LIST" )
.PP
.I SERVER
is
.B \-L
.I NAME
or
.B \-S
.IR SOCKET .
.I ACTION
is
.IR TEXT ,
.B \-
(standard input), or
.B \-\-keys
.RI ' KEY " [" KEY ...]'
(also
.BR \-k ),
in argument order.
.I WAIT-OPTS
are
.B \-\-timeout
.I S
(default 110; 0 waits without bound),
.B \-\-start\-timeout
.I S
(default 10) and
.B \-\-settle
.I S
(default 1).
.I READ-FMT
is
.B \-\-join
(logical lines, as
.BR "capture-pane \-J" )
and
.B \-\-ansi
(keep escape sequences, as
.BR "capture-pane \-e" ).
.B wait
also takes
.B \-\-agent
and
.B \-\-prompt
for
.BR "\-\-wait agent" " and " "\-\-wait prompt" .
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tmux-pane
drives one tmux pane from a script or an agent: it types text and named keys
into it, reads what it shows, and waits until the program in it is done. It
works on agent panes, which mark their turns with
.BR term-pane-mark (1),
and on plain shell panes that use the two-line prompt described in the
tmux-tools README.
.PP
It is permissive about everything except the target. It does not refuse a
busy pane, a pane with
.B synchronize-panes
on, or the caller's own pane named explicitly, and it never blocks raw
.BR "tmux send-keys" .
.SH TARGET AND SERVER
Every subcommand needs exactly one
.B \-t
naming an exact pane id,
.B %
followed by a number without leading zeros. Anything else exits 64: an empty
target, a session or window name, an index such as
.BR 0 " or " p:0.0 ,
a repeated
.BR \-t .
The pane must exist on the chosen server; otherwise the command exits 65
before doing anything. There is no fallback to the current pane or any other.
.PP
Find a pane id with
.PP
.RS
.nf
tmux list-panes -a -F '#{pane_id} #{session_name}:#{window_index}.#{pane_index} #{pane_current_command} #{pane_title}'
.fi
.RE
.PP
A pane id is exact only within one server. Without
.B \-L
or
.BR \-S ,
tmux uses the server of
.BR $TMUX ,
or, when
.B TMUX
is unset, the default socket under
.BR TMUX_TMPDIR .
Pass
.B \-L
or
.B \-S
for any other server, including a tmux nested in the pane, which the outer
server only shows as the alternate screen.
.SH SEND
.B send
runs its actions in argument order. Text is loaded into a paste buffer of its
own, named
.BI tmux-pane. PID . RANDOM . I\fR,
from a file, and pasted with
.BR "paste-buffer \-p \-d" ,
so it arrives byte for byte: a trailing
.BR ; ,
key names, tmux formats and NUL bytes are not interpreted. The paste is
bracketed when the program enabled bracketed paste, and an embedded bracket
start or end sequence is then removed;
.B \-\-type
pastes without brackets and keeps those bytes. On tmux 3.7 and later,
.B paste-buffer \-S
keeps control bytes raw.
.PP
Text from standard input
.RB ( \- )
loses exactly one trailing newline. A carriage return directly before a
newline is dropped, so CRLF input submits once per line; a lone carriage
return is kept. Input that contains NUL is passed through untouched, without
either conversion or the bracket strip. Text that starts with
.B \-
follows
.BR \-\- .
.PP
.B \-\-keys
takes tmux key names, such as
.BR "C-u" ", " "Up" " or " "M-Enter" ,
and
.B \-\-enter
adds Enter at the end. An unknown name exits 64. In the VT10x key mode, tmux
types some names literally (C-BSpace, for example), so a key without a
portable encoding exits 67 and nothing is sent. Before tmux 3.5 the key mode
is not visible, and
.B extended-keys on
counts as possibly extended: the VT10x check is skipped. With
.B synchronize-panes
on, tmux would broadcast
.BR send-keys ,
so the send turns it off for the target pane alone around its keys, in the
same command queue, then puts back the pane's own setting (or none); the
window's setting is never touched. tmux still encodes each key for the
target's own key mode.
.PP
When there are keys or
.BR \-\-enter ,
.B send
first leaves any mode the pane is in with
.BR "copy-mode \-q" ,
which discards a copy-mode selection and scroll position and also leaves
tree, clock and view modes. Text alone reaches the program without leaving
the mode.
.B \-\-keep\-mode
skips that step, and the keys go to the mode.
.PP
Everything is loaded before the sending starts, then the marker snapshot,
the mode exit, the pastes and the keys run as one tmux command queue, so two
senders cannot interleave. A send too long for one tmux command exits 64.
If the pane has input disabled
.RB ( "select-pane \-d" ),
the send exits 0 with a warning that nothing arrives.
.PP
.B send
prints nothing unless
.B \-\-print\-marker
or
.B \-\-wait
is given. With
.BR \-\-wait ,
it waits as
.B wait \-\-since
does with the marker it just took, then prints the capture, unless
.BR \-\-no\-capture .
A prompt wait's capture is the command's block; when no block finished (a
timeout, or output longer than the range) it is the range since the marker,
or the screen. With
.B \-\-lines
it is the last
.I N
rows of the range or the screen. An agent wait's capture is the range since
the marker, or the screen.
.B \-\-wait
without
.B \-\-enter
or keys prints a hint, since it waits on whatever is already running.
.SH READ
.TP
(default)
The visible screen, without trailing blank rows.
.TP
.BI \-\-lines " N"
The last
.I N
rows of the screen.
.TP
.B \-\-all
All retained history and the screen.
.TP
.BI \-\-last\-output " \fR[\fB\-\-count \fIN\fR]"
The last finished command block, or
.IR N ,
each as its
.B $ command
line followed by its output, from the last 10,000 lines. Standard error gets
.BR status=N ,
the block's exit status, or
.BR status=unknown .
.TP
.BI \-\-since " MARKER"
From the row the marker was taken at to the end, when that row can be found
exactly (see
.BR MARKERS ).
With
.BR \-\-lines ,
the last
.I N
rows of that range; with
.BR \-\-last\-output ,
the blocks that start there.
.SH MARKERS
A marker is one word, starting with
.BR tp1: ,
that records the pane's server, geometry, cursor row, hashes of nearby rows,
the typed text's first characters, the prompt identity and the agent marks.
.B send \-\-print\-marker
and
.B mark
print one;
.B send \-\-wait
reports one on its
.B result=
line. A marker from another pane or a malformed one exits 64, and one from
another or a restarted server exits 66.
.PP
.B \-\-since
is either exact or bounded; it never searches for or guesses a position.
On tmux 3.8 and later, tmux counts the history rows it drops, and the marker
row is exact while the history generation and the pane size are unchanged
and no in-place clear hid the rows above. Before 3.8, the row is trusted only
while history cannot have been trimmed (it is more than a tenth below
.BR history-limit ,
which is unchanged), has not shrunk, the size is unchanged, the first rows of
history still match, and the rows above the marker still match or a clear
scrolled them into history. Both paths then check the echo anchor: the
marker row still holds the text that was typed on it. So a
.B clear
that refills the same rows is not exact.
.PP
A marker from
.B mark
records no typed text, so only the prompt before the cursor is checked; a
.B clear
that regrows identical rows under the same prompt still passes. One case
also passes with
.B send
markers: a trim, then
.BR ESC[3J ,
then an identical refill that also reproduces the echo.
.PP
When the row cannot be found exactly, output is bounded: the visible screen,
or the last
.I N
rows with
.BR \-\-lines ,
or with
.B \-\-last\-output
every command block on the screen (the screen itself when none is whole),
never all of history. A standalone
.B read \-\-since
then exits 4 and names the reason on standard error:
.BR trimmed ,
.BR shrunk ,
.BR resized ,
.BR "grid-top changed" ,
.BR cleared ,
.BR "blank gap" ,
.BR "echo moved" ,
.BR "alternate screen" ,
.BR "generation changed" ,
.B "weak marker"
or
.B unstable
(history kept changing while it was read).
Agent TUIs usually redraw their input row after a submit, so their captures
are usually inexact: a reply that fits on one screen is still captured whole,
and a longer one needs
.B read \-\-lines
or the agent's own transcript.
.PP
The prompt identity
.RB ( user@host: )
comes from the pane title, when it has the dotfiles prompt's
.B user@host: path
form and the screen confirms it, else from the identity most prompts in the
recent history agree on, else nothing. The prompt parser is the one in
.BR tmux-copy-last-output (1),
with the same limits: output that repeats another first line, or ends in
the identity glued to text with no space, directly above a line that starts
with
.B $
or
.B %
splits a block there, and output ending in a space before a prompt whose
first line differs (path, git state, a timing suffix) is split from the next
block only at the last prompt.
.SH WAIT
Each poll is one small tmux queue every 0.2 seconds. The wait ends with 66
and
.B result=gone
if the pane dies or goes away, or its server stops or restarts.
.SS "\-\-wait agent"
Agents mark their turns through
.BR term-pane-mark (1):
.B @term_busy
while working,
.B @term_busy_seq
counting each start, and
.B @term_notify_pending
for
.BR done ,
.B waiting
(a question or a permission prompt) or
.BR blocked .
With a marker, the wait first needs the turn to start: busy set or the count
past the marker's. A new waiting exits 68 and a new blocked 69; none of these
within
.B \-\-start\-timeout
exits 70. Then it waits for busy to clear, and for
.B \-\-settle
seconds more, so a queued turn that starts at once is waited on too. A
waiting or blocked mark that was already there when the marker was taken is
ignored until it clears. With a
.B term-pane-mark
too old to count, a new
.B done
ends the start phase. Without a marker, the current marks are the baseline.
.PP
A pane already busy when the marker is taken is ambiguous: the wait may end
with that turn. An agent with a background shell can take its own idle
timeout, about a minute for Claude, to clear busy.
.SS "\-\-wait prompt"
The pane is at a prompt when the cursor is on a bare second prompt line
.RB ( $ ,
.B %
or
.BR # )
under a first line with the prompt identity, outside the alternate screen.
With a marker, a prompt counts only after progress: an exact marker with the
cursor past it, window activity after the send's second, a steady prompt
whose screen differs from the marker's, or a steady prompt after
.B \-\-settle
seconds on an unchanged screen with activity. The result's status is the
prompt's
.BR [N] ,
or 0 without one, and is the exit status. Typeahead, other prompt shapes,
remote shells and nested tmux are not supported.
.SS Resuming
After 124, or after 68 once the question is answered, run
.B wait \-\-since
with the
.B marker=
from the
.B result=
line.
.B wait \-\-capture
then prints the same capture the shortcut would.
.SH RESULT LINE
Every wait ends with one line, the last on standard error:
.PP
.RS
.nf
result=done|waiting|blocked|no-start|timeout|gone|error exact=yes|no|- status=N|unknown [reason=R] [marker=M]
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B exact
reports the capture:
.B yes
or
.B no
with a
.BR reason ,
or
.B \-
when nothing was captured. The exit status is the wait's result, never 4.
.B status=N
means the code is the command's (a command can exit 64 or 124 itself), and
.B status=unknown
means it is
.BR tmux-pane 's.
.B marker=
is present whenever a wait ran, and the capture is printed whenever it ran,
for any result except an error before or during the send.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success; for
.BR read ,
exact.
.TP
.I N
A prompt wait: the command's status.
.TP
.B 1
A tmux failure, or tmux older than 3.3.
.TP
.B 3
.B read \-\-last\-output
found no command block.
.TP
.B 4
A standalone
.B read \-\-since
was not exact; the output is bounded.
.TP
.B 64
Usage: a bad target, server, key or marker, or a marker for another pane.
.TP
.B 65
No such pane or server, before anything was done.
.TP
.B 66
The pane is dead or gone, its server is gone, or the marker is from another
or a restarted server.
.TP
.B 67
A key cannot be encoded for the target's key mode.
.TP
.B 68
The agent is waiting on a question or permission prompt.
.TP
.B 69
The agent is blocked.
.TP
.B 70
The agent's turn never started.
.TP
.B 124
The wait timed out.
.TP
.BR 130 ", " 143
Interrupted; this run's buffers and temporary files are removed.
.SH INTERFACE LIST
.B tmux-pane \-\-interface
prints one line per item, a key and its words:
.B subcommands
and their names; one line per subcommand with every option it accepts;
.B top
with the options taken without a subcommand;
.B exit
with the exit statuses;
.B result
and
.B reason
with the result kinds and inexact reasons, as the result line spells them;
.B default
with each option's default; and
.B floor
with the oldest supported tmux. The script keeps this list in one place; its
tests check the code, the usage text, this page, the README and AGENTS.md
against it, and other documentation can do the same.
.PP
.B tmux-pane \-\-check\-refs
.I FILE ...
is a maintenance aid for those documentation tests. It reads Markdown, text
or man pages and prints one line for each reference that does not match the
list: a
.B tmux-pane
subcommand that does not exist or an option it does not take, any other
long option that no subcommand takes, or an unlisted status after
.BR exit .
It ends with
.BI "#refs " N\fR,
the number of references it read, so a caller can tell a clean document from
a scan that read nothing. Check all of a repository's documents in one call:
an option used where
.B tmux-pane
is not named belongs to another command.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TMUX
Names the default server.
.TP
.B TMUX_TMPDIR
Where tmux looks for its default socket when
.B TMUX
is unset.
.TP
.B TMUX_PANE_DEBUG_DEADLINE
When 1, a wait prints its deadline.
.TP
.B TMUX_PANE_TEST_DISABLE
A comma list of features to treat as missing, for tests:
.BR paste-S ,
.BR soc ,
.BR counters ,
.BR key-mode .
.SH VERSIONS
tmux 3.3 or newer, as the server reports its
.BR #{version} ;
an older server is refused with status 1, whatever the client's version.
When the server does not answer and the client's
.B tmux \-V
is older, that is the error, also status 1: an old client may be unable to
talk to a newer server.
Features are detected directly where tmux exposes them.
A version that does not parse counts as the newest. Before 3.7,
.B paste-buffer
has no
.BR \-S .
Before 3.8, there are no history counters.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
tmux-pane send -t %7 'make test' --enter --wait prompt
M=$(tmux-pane send -t %7 'explain the failure' --enter --print-marker)
tmux-pane wait -t %7 --agent --since "$M" --capture
tmux-pane send -t %7 --keys 'C-c'
tmux-pane send -t %7 -- '-n 5'
printf 'line 1\\nline 2\\n' | tmux-pane send -t %7 - --enter
tmux-pane read -t %7 --last-output
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1),
.BR term-pane-mark (1),
.BR tmux-copy-last-output (1)
